     ('Earth facts and geomagnetism', [(M['ef'], M['moon'])], UND[8], 'Tilt <b>23.5°</b>; one spin 23 h 56 m 4.1 s, counterclockwise seen from the North Pole. <b>Aphelion = Away</b> (farthest from the Sun), <b>Perihelion = Pretty near</b>. The magnetic field flips every 2-3 lakh years and shields us from solar wind.'),
     ('The Moon', [(M['moon'], M['ecl'])], UND[9], '<b>Apogee = Away</b> (farthest), <b>Perigee = Pretty near</b>. Tidally locked: we always see one face. Origin ideas: Fission (George Darwin, no longer accepted), <b>Giant Impact</b> (Theia, a Mars-sized planet, 4.5 bn years ago), Binary Exchange Capture (proposed).'),
     ('Eclipses, latitude and longitude', [(M['ecl'], M['pq13'])], UND[10], 'Lunar eclipse: <b>S-E-M</b> (Earth in the middle, full moon). Solar eclipse: <b>S-M-E</b> (Moon in the middle, new moon). The Earth turns 1° of longitude in <b>4 minutes</b>; the east is ahead in time. A Blue Moon is not an eclipse.'),
     ('Interior of the Earth', [(M['int'], M['ipq'])], UND[11], 'Boundaries, top to bottom: <b>Conrad · Moho · Repetti · Gutenberg · Lehmann</b> = “Chai Makes Rainy Grey Lunches Lovely”. Volume: crust 0.5-1%, mantle 83%, core 16%. Lithosphere = crust + top of mantle (brittle); asthenosphere = source of magma.'),
     ('Earthquakes and seismic waves', [(M['eq'], M['em'])], UND[12], '<b>P = Passes all, S = Solids only.</b> Arrival order <b>P · S · L · R</b> = “<b>P</b>izza <b>S</b>lices <b>L</b>ook <b>R</b>ound”. Richter = magnitude (0-10); Mercalli = intensity (1-12); Moment magnitude is the one most used now.'),
     ('Earth movements and weathering', [(M['em'], M['empq'])], UND[13], '<b>O</b>rogeny = <b>O</b>nly mountains (long narrow belts); <b>E</b>peirogeny = <b>E</b>ntire continents (uplift and sinking). Exogenic work runs weathering → erosion (transport) → deposition; mass movement is gravity alone.'),
     ('Continental drift and sea-floor spreading', [(M['cd'], M['cdpq'])], UND[14], '<b>Wegener Wanders, Hess Heaves, Holmes Heats</b>: Wegener 1912 (continents drifted), Hess 1960 (sea floor spreads), Holmes 1930s (mantle convection). Proof: jigsaw fit, Ghana gold, Mesosaurus, tillite, magnetic stripes.'),
     ('Plate tectonics', [(M['pt'], M['ptpq'])], UND[15], '<b>Apart builds, together destroys, past keeps</b>: divergent = constructive, convergent = destructive, transform = conservative. No subduction, no volcano: the Himalayas have none, the Andes do.'),
     ('Volcanism', [(M['vol'], M['volpq'])], UND[16], 'Sticky (acidic, silica-rich) lava traps gas and <b>explodes</b>; runny (basic) lava <b>spreads</b> into plateaus. Eruption types: <b>Icelandic · Hawaiian · Strombolian · Vulcanian · Pelean · Plinian</b> = “<b>I</b>dli <b>H</b>ot <b>S</b>ambar <b>V</b>ada <b>P</b>ickle <b>P</b>apad”.'),
     ('Orogeny and mountains', [(M['oro'], M['rock'])], UND[17], 'By origin: <b>Residual, Fold, Fault, Volcanic</b>. <b>Horst = High</b> (uplifted block), <b>Graben = Ground sunk</b> (lowered block). Young fold mountains sit on live boundaries (Himalayas, Andes, Alps); old ones are worn (Aravallis, Appalachians).'),
     ('Rocks and the rock cycle', [(M['rock'], M['rockpq'])], UND[18], '<b>Igneous = primary</b> (from magma, no fossils); <b>Sedimentary = layers + fossils</b> (75% of the crust’s surface, only 5% of its volume); <b>Metamorphic = changed form</b> (heat and pressure). Any rock can become any other.'),
     ('Fluvial cycle: river work', [(M['ce'], M['karst'])], UND[19], 'Youth cuts a <b>V</b>alley (waterfalls), maturity bends <b>M</b>eanders, old age drops a <b>D</b>elta: “<b>V</b>ery <b>M</b>ild <b>D</b>ays”. Peneplain is the end product; headward erosion leads to river piracy.'),
     ('Karst cycle: groundwater work', [(M['karst'], M['mar'])], UND[20], '<b>Stalactite = c</b>eiling (hangs down); <b>Stalagmite = g</b>round (grows up); they join to form a column. Karst needs dense, jointed limestone, dolomite or gypsum.'),
     ('Marine cycle: waves and coasts', [(M['mar'], M['gla'])], UND[21], 'A cliff wears in order <b>Cave → Arch → Stack → Stump</b> = “<b>C</b>rabs <b>A</b>lways <b>S</b>eek <b>S</b>helter”. <b>Ria</b> = drowned river valley; <b>fjord</b> = drowned glacial valley; the Konkan is a fault coast.'),
     ('Glacial cycle: ice work', [(M['gla'], M['arid'])], UND[22], 'A river cuts a <b>V</b>, a glacier gouges a <b>U</b>. Erodes high: cirque (tarn), horn, arete, U-valley, hanging valley. Deposits low: moraine, esker, drumlin, kettle, outwash plain.'),
